nts by modeling their effect on the solution of the forward problem using the central limit theorem. The solid curve approximates the Cramér-Rao lower bound (4.44). S = 2.… 】\n\n\nFine scale uncertainty in parameter estimation for\n\nelliptic equations\n\nJames Nolen∗George Papanicolaou†\n\nSeptember 4, 2009\n\nAbstract\n\nWe study the problem of estimating the coeﬃcients in an ellip-\n\ntic partial diﬀerential equation using noisy measurements of a solu-\n\ntion to the equation. Although the unknown coeﬃcients may vary\n\non many scales we aim only at estimating their slowly varying parts,\n\nthus reducing the complexity of the inverse problem. However, ignor-\n\ning the ﬁne-scale ﬂuctuations altogether introduces uncertainty in the\n\nestimates, even in the absence of measurement noise. We propose a\n\nstrategy for quantifying the uncertainty due to the ﬁne scale ﬂuctua-\n\ntions in the coeﬃcients by modeling their eﬀect on the solution of the\n\nforward problem using the central limit theorem. When this is possi-\n\nble, the Bayesian estimation of the coeﬃcients reduces to a weighted\n\nleast squares problem with a covariance matrix whose rank is low re-\n\ngardless of the number of measurements and does not depend on the\n\ndetails of the coeﬃcient ﬂuctuations.\n\n1 Introduction\n\nWe study the problem of estimating the coeﬃcients of elliptic partial dif-\n\nferential equations using noisy measurements of solutions. We ﬁrst consider\n\nthe equation\n\n−∆u+a(x)u=f(x), x ∈D. (1.1)\n\nThe domain D⊂R d is a smooth bounded region, and we impose a Dirichlet\n\nboundary condition on ∂D. The equation models diﬀusion in a random\n\n∗Department of Mathematics, Duke University, Box 90320 Durham, NC 27708\n\n(nolen@math.duke.edu).\n\n†Department of Mathematics, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305 (papan-\n\nico@math.stanford.edu).\n\n1\n\npotential. Later we also consider the estimation of an unknown diﬀusion\n\ncoeﬃcient but only in one dimension:\n\n−(a(x)u x)x+bu =f(